We need to refer to old values. At method start, size is initial size. The method sets value at given index, returns previous value. The spec: If index is valid (non-negative and less than initial size), then for every index > index && index < size (i.e., from index+1 to size-1) the element at that index remains unchanged unless it was initially null. Means after operation, for each i where index < i < old size, we have either old element was null, then maybe changed? Actually "remains unchanged unless it was initially null" means if old element was not null, then it stays same. If old element was null, we don't assert anything (could change). So condition: for all i; index < i && i < \old(this.size()); (\old(this.get(i)) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))). Also need premise that index valid: 0 <= index && index < \old(this.size()). So assert: (0 <= index && index < \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; index < i && i < \old(this.size()); (\old(this.get(i)) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)));

Make sure we use this.get(i).equals(...). Use .equals for object comparison.

Thus final assert statement.

Return as code block.

