analysisWe need to produce an assert statement that reflects the comment.

Comment: 
```
// @@@ If the index is valid (non-negative and less than the initial size of the list), then for every index greater than the specified index and less than the size of the list, the element at that index remains unchanged unless it was initially null.
```

We are inside method set(int index, E obj). Preconditions: checkInterval ensures index valid (0 <= index <= size-1). The comment says: If the index is valid (non-negative and less than the initial size of the list), then for every index greater than the specified index and less than the size of the list, the element at that index remains unchanged unless it was initially null.

We need to express: For all i such that index < i && i < size (initial size), the element after operation at i equals the element before operation at i, unless the element before was null (i.e., if old element was null, maybe it can change? The comment says "remains unchanged unless it was initially null". So if old element was null, the new element may be something else, but we don't assert equality in that case.

Thus specification: (index >=0 && index < \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; index < i && i < \old(this.size()); (\old(this.get(i)) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)));

But need to use equality semantics: maybe use == for null, .equals for non-null. Use this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i))? If they are objects, we can use .equals. We need to ensure not null before calling equals. So we can assert: (\old(this.get(i)) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))) . That's fine.

Also need to refer to size before modification: \old(this.size()).

Thus final assert: 
assert (index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; index < i && i < \old(this.size()); (\old(this.get(i)) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)));

Make sure parentheses correct.

