Interpretation: Preconditions: index is valid (non-negative and < initial size). The method sets element at index to obj, returns previous. The spec: for every i where index < i < size (initial size), the element at that index after operation remains unchanged unless it was initially null. Means: If the old element at i was not null, then after set it remains equal to old element. If old element was null, maybe it could become something else? The spec says "remains unchanged unless it was initially null." So if old element was null, we don't assert anything (could change). So we assert: for all i; index < i && i < \old(this.size()); (\old(this.get(i)) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))). Need to use \old for size as well? The size may stay same after set. So size unchanged. But we can reference \old(this.size()).

Thus assert: index >=0 && index < \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index < i && i < \old(this.size()); (\old(this.get(i)) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)));

But also we need to ensure index valid condition is part of implication? The comment says: "If the index is valid (non-negative and less than the initial size of the list), then ..." So we should condition on index validity. So final assert could be:

